course: dictionary
lesson: 12284
title: When a word hints at a trick
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Conveniently has two meanings. The first is easy and handy: «The shop is conveniently close.» The shop is near, so it is easy to reach.
explain: The second meaning is said with doubt, as if someone planned it. «He conveniently forgot his homework.» Did he really forget, or did it suit him a little too well? The word tells you to be suspicious.
explain: To find which meaning is used, read the words around it. Is something useful and easy, or does someone benefit a little too much? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Conveniently means in a way that is easy, useful or suitable.
fact: Conveniently can also mean at a time that suits someone a little too well, as if on purpose.
fact: In stories, the second meaning is often a hint that a character is pretending or lying.
fact: Useful phrases: «conveniently located», «conveniently placed», «conveniently forgot».
fact: «She conveniently lost the letter» suggests she did not want to answer it.
exercise: «The bus stop is conveniently placed right outside our school.» What does conveniently mean here? | *In a way that is easy and handy | Angrily | At a time that suits someone too well, on purpose | Very quickly
exercise: «Tom conveniently forgot his homework again.» What does the word conveniently hint at? | That Tom has a lot of spare homework | *That Tom may not really have forgotten, as if on purpose | That Tom is very clever and never forgets
exercise: Which sentence uses conveniently in its first, easy meaning? | «He conveniently fell asleep in class.» | «She conveniently lost the letter.» | *«The café is conveniently close to the station.»
test: «The park is conveniently placed next to our house.» What does conveniently mean? | *Handily, in an easy and useful way | Rudely | Lazily
test: «Mia conveniently found a reason to leave when the washing-up started.» What does this suggest? | She found a shiny new reason to be happy | She cleaned up very quickly | *She may have left on purpose to avoid the job
exam: «The bus was conveniently late, so Sam could skip the spelling test.» What does the word conveniently suggest? | *The lateness suited Sam too well, as if he planned it | Sam was lucky and it was simply an accident | The bus was early and Sam was annoyed
